From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ole Algoritme <olealgoritme@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 13:43:06 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] nak: compute lanes that leave early exit, the rest runs
 convergent (NAK_EARLY_EXIT)

Most compute shaders end in one of two shapes: the whole body inside
"if (in_bounds) { ... }", or an early return that the front-end turned
into a loop that never iterates ("loop { if (oob) { ...; break; } ...;
break; }", as vkd3d-proton's shaders have).  Either way the body sits in
divergent control flow, and NAK never puts the values of divergent blocks
in uniform registers, so these shaders run without the uniform datapath:
every descriptor index, handle and root-constant address is computed per
lane in GPRs.  In the Steel Nomad compute shader 88825d04 (8x8, 1920x1080)
only UR0-UR5 were used at all, against UR0-UR45 in the reference
compiler's code for the same shader, which exits the out-of-bounds lanes
(@P0 BRA to EXIT) and runs the rest with uniform registers.

nak_nir_lower_early_exit, before nak_nir_lower_cf in compute shaders:

 - a trailing loop whose header has no back edge loses its breaks (they
   become halts, EXIT) and its body moves out of it;
 - a trailing if with a divergent condition keeps its smaller side in the
   if, followed by "if (cond) halt" (or the inverse), and the larger side
   moves after it to the top level.  The guarded side reconverges (its
   own BSSY/BSYNC) before the exit, so no lane runs it while the others run
   the code after it.  Only when the guarded side is small next to the
   other (at most 16 instructions or a quarter of it);
 - past the exit, and inside the guarded side, the condition is a known
   constant: later bounds checks on it fold away.

Reaching the end of a compute shader is the same as exiting, and halted
lanes never block a later sync, so nothing else changes: subgroup ops see
the same active lanes, and a barrier after a partial early return was
undefined before as well.  Shaders with compute derivatives are skipped.

With more code convergent, three passes that ran on the old shapes run
again or see the new ones: nak_nir_opt_uniform_address runs again for the
newly convergent blocks (it skips accesses it already split); the
rematerializer accepts a UGPR pair of constant-buffer copies as the base
of a constant load, as it does a pinned pair; and divergent loops at the
top level keep pinned uniform address bases, as they had inside the
divergent if.

Results (GB202 / SM120, NVK under drm-shim):

  88825d04 (Steel Nomad, 8x8): 168 -> 128 GPRs (12 -> 16 warps/SM, as
    the reference compiler's 128), instructions 2245 -> 2292
  fb2df990: 56 -> 48 GPRs, 593 -> 674 instructions
  94b6df80: 48 GPRs, 553 -> 572 instructions
  compute corpus (115 shaders): GPRs -3.5%, warps/SM +1.5%,
    instructions +0.9% (mostly uniform-datapath address math), no memory
    spills added

On an RTX 5090 with the captured Steel Nomad inputs, compute shader
88825d04 runs 17% faster (2230 -> 1861 us; 168 -> 128 GPRs) with
bit-identical outputs.  Steel Nomad scores +2% (73.8 / 72.9 fps against
72.2 / 71.2 fps).

On by default.  NAK_EARLY_EXIT=0 restores the previous structure (the
divergent if or the loop around the whole body).  The knob is bit 17 of
nak_knobs_key(), so shaders cached with the knob off and on have
different keys.
